course: dictionary
lesson: 4875
title: Ideally and the perfect wish
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Ideally» is a small word that changes the meaning of a sentence. It means in the best possible way, or the way you would most like if everything were perfect. «Ideally, I'd like a puppy.» The speaker is saying what they would most like, even though it may not happen.
explain: «Ideally» often shows a wish or a goal. In «...promising to try to keep warming well under 2 degrees and ideally 1.5», the word tells us that 1.5 degrees is the best goal, the perfect target. The sentence then adds that news reports say the world is not yet on track to reach it.
explain: «Ideally» can also mean exactly right for something. «The house is ideally placed near the school» means it is in the perfect spot for getting to school.
explain: To check the meaning, swap «ideally» for «if everything were perfect» and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Ideally means in the best possible way, or the way you would most like if everything were perfect.
fact: «Ideally» can also mean perfectly suited or exactly right for something, as in «ideally placed».
fact: Phrases to spot: «ideally suited», «ideally located», and «ideally, we would…».
fact: In science and maths, ideally can mean in a perfect model, ignoring small problems of the real world.
fact: «Ideally» is an adverb, so it tells you more about how something is wanted or done.
exercise: «Ideally, I'd like a puppy.» What does ideally mean here? | *In the best possible way, if everything were perfect | Only just | Sadly, with tears | Very quickly
exercise: «The house is ideally placed near the school.» What does ideally suggest? | Painted in bright colours | Far away from everything | *Exactly right for the spot
exercise: In «...promising to try to keep warming well under 2 degrees and ideally 1.5», which word shows the best goal? | promising | signed | warming | *ideally
test: Which sentence uses ideally to show a wish? | Tom painted the fence blue. | *Ideally, we would have sunshine for the picnic. | The dog ate the sandwich. | The bus left at nine o'clock.
test: Ella says «ideally» means the same as «maybe». What is the best reply? | *It means the best possible way, so it is not the same as maybe. | It means at the end of something. | It means very loud. | She is right, because both words are guesses.
exam: Which check helps you find the meaning of ideally in a sentence? | Does it start with a capital letter? | *Would «if everything were perfect» still make sense? | Is the word long? | Is it a noun?
